          "def-measure-with-density",
          "thm-indefinite-integral-of-a-nonnegative-function-is-a-measure"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-multivariate-normal-law": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-standard-normal-and-normal-laws": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-real-exponential-function-and-e": "mathematical-premise",
          "cor-exponential-reciprocal-and-positivity": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-exponential-is-strictly-increasing": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-axiom-of-choice": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-measure-with-density": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-indefinite-integral-of-a-nonnegative-function-is-a-measure": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "dependency_level": 0,
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "library": "mathematics",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "David Skinner, Advanced Quantum Field Theory, Chapter 2, “QFT in Zero Dimensions” (University of Cambridge Part III lecture notes)",
              "url": "https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/dbs26/AQFT/chap2.pdf"
            },
            {
              "title": "Nima Moshayedi, Quantum Field Theory and Functional Integrals (lecture notes, arXiv:1902.08652)",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/1902.08652"
            }
          ]
        },
        "justified_by": [
          "thm-qft-finite-euclidean-polynomial-measure"
        ],
        "proof_strategy": "direct",
        "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $N\\ge1$ be finite and let $C$ be a real symmetric positive-semidefinite $N\\times N$ matrix; positive-semidefinite means $u^{\\mathsf T}Cu\\ge0$ for every $u\\in\\mathbb R^N$. The centered multivariate normal Borel probability law with covariance $C$ is $\\gamma_C:=N_N(0,C)$ as defined by [[def-multivariate-normal-law]]: every linear projection $q\\mapsto u^{\\mathsf T}q$ has scalar normal law $N(0,u^{\\mathsf T}Cu)$, and the law exists even when $C$ is singular. Here $N(0,\\sigma^2)$ is the law specified in [[def-standard-normal-and-normal-laws]], with density $e^{-x^2/2}/\\sqrt{2\\pi}$ for the standard case.\n\nLet $U:\\mathbb R^N\\to[0,\\infty)$ be a real homogeneous quartic polynomial, meaning $U$ is a polynomial and $U(sq)=s^4U(q)$ for all $s\\in\\mathbb R$ and $q\\in\\mathbb R^N$. For a coupling $\\lambda\\ge0$, define the partition integral and its weighted measure by\n\n$$Z_{C,U}(\\lambda):=\\int_{\\mathbb R^N}e^{-\\lambda U(q)}\\,d\\gamma_C(q),\\qquad \\nu_{C,U,\\lambda}(A):=\\int_Ae^{-\\lambda U(q)}\\,d\\gamma_C(q)$$\n\nfor Borel $A\\subseteq\\mathbb R^N$. Whenever $0<Z_{C,U}(\\lambda)<\\infty$, the **finite Euclidean interacting measure** is the Borel probability measure\n\n$$d\\mu_{C,U,\\lambda}(q):=Z_{C,U}(\\lambda)^{-1}e^{-\\lambda U(q)}\\,d\\gamma_C(q).$$\n\nFor a polynomial $P:\\mathbb R^N\\to\\mathbb R$ with finite integral, write $\\langle P\\rangle_{C,U,\\lambda}:=\\int P\\,d\\mu_{C,U,\\lambda}$. The exact supplier [[thm-qft-finite-euclidean-polynomial-measure]] proves that $Z_{C,U}(\\lambda)$ is positive and finite and that every polynomial insertion is integrable. This is a Borel measure on finite-dimensional $\\mathbb R^N$; it is not a measure on distributions on continuum Euclidean spacetime and does not by itself assert reflection positivity or reconstruction of a continuum field."
      },
      {
        "id": "lem-qft-finite-gaussian-wick-moments",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "title": "Finite Gaussian normalization and Wick moments",
        "deps": [
          "def-qft-finite-euclidean-interacting-measure",
          "def-multivariate-normal-law",
          "def-standard-normal-and-normal-laws",
          "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "lem-characteristic-function-of-a-multivariate-normal-law",
          "lem-moments-give-derivatives-of-the-characteristic-function",
          "thm-generalized-holder-inequality-for-products",
          "thm-of-square-roots",
          "thm-gaussian-integral",
          "thm-substitution-for-improper-integrals",
          "def-real-exponential-function-and-e"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-qft-finite-euclidean-interacting-measure": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-multivariate-normal-law": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-standard-normal-and-normal-laws": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-axiom-of-choice": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-characteristic-function-of-a-multivariate-normal-law": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-moments-give-derivatives-of-the-characteristic-function": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-generalized-holder-inequality-for-products": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-of-square-roots": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-gaussian-integral": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-substitution-for-improper-integrals": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-real-exponential-function-and-e": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "dependency_level": 1,
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "library": "mathematics",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "David Skinner, Advanced Quantum Field Theory, Chapter 2, “QFT in Zero Dimensions” (University of Cambridge Part III lecture notes)",
              "url": "https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/dbs26/AQFT/chap2.pdf"
            },
            {
              "title": "Nima Moshayedi, Quantum Field Theory and Functional Integrals (lecture notes, arXiv:1902.08652)",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/1902.08652"
            }
          ]
        },
        "proof_strategy": "direct",
        "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $N\\ge1$ be finite, let $C$ be a real symmetric positive-semidefinite $N\\times N$ covariance matrix, and let $Q\\sim\\gamma_C=N_N(0,C)$ be the law of [[def-qft-finite-euclidean-interacting-measure]]. For $k\\ge0$ and vectors $u_1,\\ldots,u_k\\in\\mathbb R^N$, every product $\\prod_{j=1}^k(u_j^{\\mathsf T}Q)$ is integrable. For $k=0$ its expectation is $1$. If $k$ is odd, its expectation is $0$; if $k=2m$, then\n\n$$\\mathbb E\\!\\left[\\prod_{j=1}^{2m}u_j^{\\mathsf T}Q\\right]=\\sum_{\\pi\\in\\operatorname{Pair}(2m)}\\prod_{\\{a,b\\}\\in\\pi}u_a^{\\mathsf T}Cu_b,$$\n\nwhere $\\operatorname{Pair}(2m)$ is the finite set of partitions of $\\{1,\\ldots,2m\\}$ into unordered two-element subsets. Thus every polynomial in $Q$ has a finite absolute moment, computed by finitely many covariance pairings."
      },
      {
        "id": "thm-qft-finite-euclidean-polynomial-measure",
        "kind": "theorem",
        "title": "Actual finite Euclidean polynomial measure and moments",
        "deps": [
